/* Iran in World Politics — the course skin.
 *
 * The general edition is a national-popular quiz show: warm gold plate, the flag
 * down the stage edges as lighting, a red burn on the live tile. This edition is
 * the same show moved into a seminar room, and the room is the module — so the
 * palette is the one a university press would print it in: deep blue for what
 * the board marks right, copper for what it marks wrong, and a colder glass for
 * every panel the player sits behind.
 *
 * Nothing here moves the layout. The show's identity resolves to the accent
 * block at the top of `:root` plus three neutral panels, so a skin is mostly a
 * list of colours; the only structural additions are the week numbers on the
 * title card and the module line at the foot of it.
 *
 * Every rule is keyed to `html[data-edition="course"]`, which the registry sets
 * before the edition event fires. The general edition reaches none of this.
 */

/* ── The room ───────────────────────────────────────────────
   `--glass`, `--glass-hi` and `--card` are deliberately not part of the show's
   accent block — the engine calls them the room, not the show. A second edition
   is a second room, and these three panels cover every surface the player reads
   from: the green room's plates, the title card, the board's cells, the lobby's
   panels. Cooling them to a navy is what makes this edition legible as a
   different venue before anything else on the screen is repainted. */
html[data-edition="course"] {
  --glass:    rgba(7, 12, 28, 0.52);
  --glass-hi: rgba(17, 27, 52, 0.80);
  --card:     rgba(11, 18, 38, 0.84);
}

/* ── The show ───────────────────────────────────────────────
   Same six forms per hue as the engine's block. Repainting the accents carries
   the lit tile, the live tile, the correct and wrong panels, the clock, the
   score alerts and the two washes down the stage edges with it. */
html[data-edition="course"] {
  /* right */
  --green:          #2456c8;
  --green-rgb:      36, 86, 200;
  --green-lit:      #93b4ff;
  --green-lit-rgb:  147, 180, 255;
  --green-rim:      86, 132, 224;
  --green-deep:     9, 20, 52;

  /* wrong */
  --red:            #d2762a;
  --red-rgb:        210, 118, 42;
  --red-lit:        #ffbe86;
  --red-lit-rgb:    255, 190, 134;
  --red-sheen:      #ffcf9b;
  --red-deep:       48, 26, 9;
  --red-deep-card:  52, 28, 10;
  --red-alert:      #ffa96b;
  --red-clock:      #ffc088;
  --red-clock-dim:  #e6bb9a;
  --red-on-tile:    #f3e2d0;
}

/* ── The stage ──────────────────────────────────────────────
   Swapped by path, not by tint: the engine's own `assets/stage-backdrop.png`
   must keep serving the general edition, so this edition ships a second file
   and claims it here. The `::before` / `::after` washes down the frame's edges
   need no rule of their own — they already resolve to the accents above, which
   is why the flag-lit margins turn blue and copper for free. */
html[data-edition="course"] .stage-bg {
  background-image: url("assets/stage-backdrop-course.png");
}

/* The render came back darker than the engine's own art in the one place that
   matters: both have the same median luma, but this one's brightest pixel is
   barely half the general backdrop's (`p99` 88 against 181). The engine's edge
   washes are documented as fallbacks precisely because the general art carries
   its own lights, so on this file they were doing the lighting alone and the
   stage read as a black field. Lifted here rather than baked into the PNG, so
   the render stays as generated and the correction is one number to tune. */
html[data-edition="course"] .stage-bg {
  filter: brightness(1.55) saturate(1.12);
}

/* ── The wordmark ───────────────────────────────────────────
   `edition.js` swaps the path on `editionchange` and this edition ships a
   cut-out, so the engine's own `drop-shadow` — drawn for the general edition's
   cut-out — still applies and is left alone. No rule is needed here. The art
   arrived on an opaque field once and a `mix-blend-mode: screen` carried it;
   that blend is gone with the field, and putting it back on a cut-out would
   eat the engraving's light against the dark stage. */

/* ── The title card ─────────────────────────────────────────
   The two rails flanking the plate are the module's ten taught weeks, in
   seminar order: weeks 1-5 down the left margin, 6-10 down the right. Numbering
   them is the whole design cue — an unnumbered rail is a word cloud, a numbered
   one is a syllabus — and it costs no markup, because the engine's rails are
   already two ordered lists of exactly five and five. */
html[data-edition="course"] #screen-splash .rail-list { counter-reset: week 0; }
html[data-edition="course"] #screen-splash .rail-right .rail-list { counter-reset: week 5; }

html[data-edition="course"] #screen-splash .rail-list li {
  display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.72em;
}
/* RTL puts the numeral on the reading edge automatically: a flex row starts at
   the right in a right-to-left context, so the week number leads the Persian
   line the same way it leads the English one. */
html[data-edition="course"] #screen-splash .rail-list li::before {
  counter-increment: week;
  content: counter(week, decimal-leading-zero);
  flex: none;
  color: rgba(147, 180, 255, 0.55);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
}

/* The module line at the foot of the title card: code, title, institution, the
   way a course handbook sets it. Two `content` values rather than one, because
   `content` is not translatable and the Persian line has to switch faces and
   lose the two Latin-only conventions — the same treatment the language pills
   and the edition tiles get. */
html[data-edition="course"] #screen-splash .plate::after {
  content: "IR4595 · Iran in World Politics · University of St Andrews";
  position: absolute; left: 50%; bottom: 3.1%; transform: translateX(-50%);
  font-size: clamp(7.5px, 0.6vw, 10.5px);
  letter-spacing: var(--caps); text-transform: uppercase;
  white-space: nowrap; text-shadow: var(--halo);
  color: rgba(147, 180, 255, 0.52);
  pointer-events: none;
}
html[data-edition="course"][dir="rtl"] #screen-splash .plate::after {
  content: "IR4595 · ایران در سیاست جهانی · دانشگاه سنت اندروز";
  font-family: var(--fa); font-size: clamp(9px, 0.72vw, 12px);
  letter-spacing: 0; text-transform: none;
}

/* ── The board ──────────────────────────────────────────────
   The general edition's cells are warm glass with a warm grey numeral. This
   edition's are colder and flatter: a lapis hairline, a numeral pulled toward
   the same blue, and a hover that reads as a highlighter passing over a line of
   text rather than a tile being lit from behind. The live cell and the Daily
   Double need no rule — they burn in `--red`, which is now copper. */
html[data-edition="course"] .tile { border-color: rgba(147, 180, 255, 0.11); }
html[data-edition="course"] .tile:hover:not(:disabled) {
  background: rgba(13, 24, 50, 0.88);
  border-color: rgba(147, 180, 255, 0.34);
}
html[data-edition="course"] .tile .amt { color: #b6c4e4; }
